/* ============================================================================
 * fog_poc.c — Silent Hill 1 fog + snow PoC, GLTF-native edition
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * A self-contained C99 software renderer that loads a modern GLB (glTF 2.0)
 * DIRECTLY in-app (no pre-extracted assets — the room renders purely through
 * the GLTF pipeline) and applies the authentic Silent Hill PS1 engine fog and
 * snow effects, replicated verbatim from the silent-hill-decomp sources, with
 * the exact CamTrack Editor (camdat.html) camera system.
 *
 * TARGET CONSTRAINT: RK3326 / Mali-G31 MP2 handhelds run EmulationStation with
 * Desktop GL 2.1 / OpenGLES 1.0 (fixed function only). This renderer is a
 * pure-C software pipeline that uses NO OpenGL at all (the presentation layer
 * is GDI on Windows, SDL2/X11 on Linux) — it therefore cannot depend on any
 * GL 3+ core-profile feature and runs anywhere, GLES 1.0 targets included.
 * All engine math is integer/fixed-point exactly like the PS1 GTE/GPU.
 *
 * GROUND TRUTH SOURCES (engine math replicated line-for-line):
 *  - src/bodyprog/gfx/bodyprog_80055028.c
 *      WorldEnv_FogDistanceSet()   -> fogRamp LUT + depthShift   [FOG LUT]
 *      func_800559A8()             -> Q12 fog-factor LUT interp  [FOG BASE]
 *      func_80055A50()             -> OTZ fog lookup (snow prims)[FOG LOOKUP]
 *      WorldGfx_2dEffectsDraw()    -> fog background = clear color
 *  - src/bodyprog/gfx/bodyprog_80056D8C.c
 *      Gfx_MeshDraw() path B (field_0==0, fog enabled):
 *        per vertex:  T = clamp(4096 - ramp(zQ8)*16 - intensity, 0, 4096)
 *        textured GT4 vertex color = tintEff * T   (DPCS toward black)
 *        fog overlay G4 color      = fogColor * (4096-T)/4096 (drawn on top)
 *        -> final pixel = (texel * tintEff*T)>>7 + fogColor*f   [additive veil]
 *      func_80057B7C()             -> per-vertex fogVert = ramp[(sz<<7)>>shift]
 *  - src/bodyprog/world/world_effects.c + src/bodyprog/sys/map_info.c
 *      MAP_EFFECTS_INFOS[1] THR outdoor preset: fog on, fogDistance Q12(13.5),
 *      fogColor {108,100,116}, worldTint Q12(0.95/1.00/1.08),
 *      worldLightIntensity Q12(1.1) -> tintEff (133,140,151)
 *  - src/maps/particle.c (map0_s00 build = THR outdoor street snow):
 *      Particle_SystemUpdate / Particle_SnowInitialize / Particle_Update /
 *      Particle_Spawn / Particle_SpawnMovementApply / Particle_MovementUpdate /
 *      Particle_PositionRandomize / sharedFunc_800CFFF8 / Particle_SnowDraw /
 *      Particle_CameraMovedCheck — the whole environmental snow system 1:1.
 *      Snow sprite + 8 distance-gradient CLUTs from the ORIGINAL BG_ETC.TIM
 *      are EMBEDDED in the executable (src/snow_tim_data.h, generated by
 *      tools/extract_snow_tim.py; engine placement VRAM (768,0), tpage 44).
 *  - src/main/rng.c — LCG seed*1664525+1013904223, Rand16 = seed>>17
 *  - camtrack.html (CamTrack Editor v2 — supersedes camdat.html) — the
 *      COMPLETE camera reference: evalTrack() (line/circle/curve/fixed/
 *      polyline/spiral/zigzag/figure8, lookMode path/normal/point/fixed/
 *      center, loopMode loop/pingPong/once), phase01()/tri01(), angleAt(),
 *      catmullRom()/catmullRomTangent()/curveLUT()/curveParamAt() (64-sample
 *      chord table + monotone cubic Hermite Fritsch-Carlson refinement),
 *      pointsLUT()/polyParamAt()/polyPosAt()/polyTangentAt() + zigzagPoints()
 *      (exact polyline/zigzag segment walk), the spiral/figure8 analytic
 *      tangents, normalFromTangent(), evTargetQuat() (three.js
 *      Matrix4.lookAt / Euler YXZ), the turn filter
 *      (updateTrackCam/turnStepLimited: bounded-rate 160 deg/s slerp toward
 *      the exact track orientation while playing, snap on jump/track-switch/
 *      pause), tickPlayback timing (t += min(dt,0.1)*rate, t %=
 *      cycleDuration) and pipCam projection (PerspectiveCamera
 *      fov=track.fovDeg, near 0.05, far 100). Per-model camdat flags
 *      fogon / nighton / flashlighton are parsed (camtrack.html defines them
 *      as data for the consuming program; only fogon drives rendering here).
 *  - camtrack_v2 (the World & Weather editor) — v13: the per-model env
 *      object (models[].env) is parsed and drives the boot environment:
 *      pair [A,B] (Gfx_MapEnvSet at boot, superseding the nighton-derived
 *      pair), weatherTarget (sharedFunc_800D0B18 at boot), bootWeather
 *      (Particle_EnvironmentSet at load — the editor's setBootWeather,
 *      applied unconditionally incl. recipe 2), lensFlare, flashlightOn,
 *      psxWobble (--modern when false), dither, particles (--nosnow when
 *      false), engine (--nofog when false), animsDemo (--anims-demo when
 *      true: the cutscene/event-gated drivers play — the wallpaper's
 *      demo scenes set it per scene; absent leaves the CLI default).
 *      Present-field semantics mirror
 *      the editor's sanitizeEnv whitelist verbatim (strict booleans,
 *      clamps 0..20 / 0..8 / -1..6 / 0..5). Priority: CLI flags > camdat
 *      env > camdat legacy flags > engine defaults — the editor's own
 *      documented precedence (URL/CLI params override the saved env).
 *      Legacy camdat.json files WITHOUT env parse EXACTLY as in v12.
 *
 * PSX WOBBLE (authentic GTE vertex jitter) — keybind P, DEFAULT ON (v7):
 *   The PS1 GTE (RTPS/RTPT) produces 16-bit INTEGER screen coordinates
 *   (the fractional part of the fixed-point projection is TRUNCATED away —
 *   DuckStation gte.cpp:786 PushSXY(s32(Sx >> 16)), no rounding),
 *   so every vertex lands on the integer raster of the console framebuffer
 *   (320x240 — SCREEN_WIDTH/SCREEN_HEIGHT, game.h) each frame; sub-pixel
 *   camera motion therefore makes vertices snap between pixels — the
 *   classic PS1 polygon wobble. When enabled, this PoC truncates all
 *   projected vertices onto that same engine 320x240 raster (x on the
 *   fb_w/320 grid, y on the fb_h/240 grid, mapped to the render
 *   resolution); affine texture mapping + 15-bit framebuffer
 *   quantization are part of the same authentic GTE/GPU package (the PS1
 *   hardware always does all three together).
 *
 * NO ASSET EXTRACTION: the room/geometry/textures load straight from the GLB
 * at runtime (embedded JSON + BIN chunks, PNG textures decoded in-app with a
 * self-contained inflate). Nothing is extracted from the GLTF to external
 * files. The only non-GLTF assets are the snow sprite/CLUT halfwords baked
 * into the binary (engine BG_ETC.TIM particle data) and the camdat.json
 * camera track (the camera data file, not a scene asset).
 * ==========================================================================*/

